  "query_preview": "/MONDAY THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 306 PM CDT Fri Sep 25 2026\n\nAs mentioned above in the short term discussion, an upper level \ntrough continues to deepen across the western United States on \nMonday, bringing a strong fetch of Pacific moisture across the \nregion, with some potential to bring moisture off of Hurricane \nOdalys as well. Additionally, as Hurricane Polo is expected to track \nacross the Baja Peninsula, we may get an additional surge of Pacific \nmoisture. At the surface, a lee side cyclone develops off the Front \nRange, slowly tracking from eastern Colorado into southwest \nNebraska. This should bring another chance for precipitation across \nthe region, with fairly strong chances of wetting precipitation \nacross the region. In fact, both the European and GEFS ensembles \nbring at least a 30 to 40 percent chance for 24 hour rainfall to \nexceed half an inch Monday night into Tuesday afternoon across most \nof the region. \n\nUncertainty begins to build into the forecast by Tuesday, as \nguidance struggles to handle the interaction of Hurricane Polo and \nthe upper level trough. Polo is expected to make its turn to the \nnortheast on Monday, tracking across the Baja Peninsula by Tuesday. \nAs the upper level trough tracking across the western United States \npushes east, expecting that it should begin to interact with \nHurricane Polo at some point on Tuesday. As the timing is still \nsomewhat in question, as well as the intensity of the tropical \nsystem, guidance really struggles to come to a consensus on the \nsolution. Even a look at the GEFS spaghetti plots reveals a fair \namount of uncertainty within the GEFS ensemble on how the upper \nlevel jet interacts with the hurricane. This also leaves a bit of \nuncertainty between the GEFS and European ensembles, as both \nensembles struggle with the upper level interaction. All this to \nsay, forecast confidence remains on the lower end at this time, \nespecially concerning potential precipitation placement and timing. \nFor what it is worth, there is some consensus on rainfall occurring \nto our south, across Kansas, as well as some to our east. Whether or \nnot precipitation makes it further west into portions of the \nSandhills remains low confidence. However, there is some suggestion \nthat we may see some precipitation out of this system, given the \nexpected surge of moisture. Will obviously be keeping a close eye on \nthe evolution of this system and forecast trends through the \nweekend, as this will largely impact our weather beyond Tuesday. \n\nThough there is lower confidence mid week onward given the \npotentially complex upper level flow, for now, guidance continues to \nsuggest more seasonal high temperatures to persist mid to late week. \nRain chances remain in the forecast, but again, remain low \nconfidence given the uncertainty in the jet stream interaction with \nthe hurricane. For now, expecting temperatures to remain around the \nupper 60s to lower 70s, which is right around average for this time \nof year. In fact, late September into early October, our average \nhighs are around 72 degrees, with an average low around 42. However, \nwhile highs are expected to remain around average, lows are expected \nto remain above average, with lows mostly remaining in the 50s.",
